---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout
  title="The Phone That Killed The Compact Flagship — KILBURN"
  description="Three weeks with the Aspect Mini Pro. Deep-dive companion, Ep. 247."
>
  <section class="pt-32 pb-12 px-6" data-accent="red">
    <div class="max-w-[1600px] mx-auto grid lg:grid-cols-2 gap-10 lg:gap-16">
      <div class="lg:sticky lg:top-24 lg:self-start">
        <div class="video-player-shell">
          <div class="video-player-screen">
            <img src="https://picsum.photos/seed/reviewvid/1280/720" alt="Video still" />
            <div class="absolute top-4 left-4 bg-black/80 backdrop-blur px-3 py-2 text-[10px] font-mono uppercase tracking-widest flex items-center gap-2 z-10">
              <span class="live-dot"></span>Reading Mode · Video Pinned
            </div>
            <div class="absolute top-4 right-4 bg-black/80 backdrop-blur px-3 py-2 text-[10px] font-mono text-white z-10">
              06:43 / 18:24
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="video-controls">
            <div class="video-progress">
              <div class="video-progress-fill"></div>
            </div>
            <div class="flex items-center justify-between text-white text-xs font-mono">
              <span>06:43 / 18:24</span>
              <span>Ep. 247</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="mt-6">
          <div class="text-[10px] font-mono uppercase tracking-widest text-[var(--fg-dim)] mb-3">
            Chapters
          </div>
          <div class="space-y-1">
            <button class="chapter active">
              <span>00:00 — Intro: the small phone problem</span>
              <i class="fas fa-play text-[10px] text-[var(--accent)]"></i>
            </button>
            <button class="chapter text-[var(--fg-dim)]">
              <span>02:14 — Build &amp; in-hand feel</span>
              <span class="text-[10px] text-[var(--fg-mute)] font-mono">02:14</span>
            </button>
            <button class="chapter text-[var(--fg-dim)]">
              <span>05:48 — The display reality check</span>
              <span class="text-[10px] text-[var(--fg-mute)] font-mono">05:48</span>
            </button>
            <button class="chapter text-[var(--fg-dim)]">
              <span>08:32 — Battery: three-week data</span>
              <span class="text-[10px] text-[var(--fg-mute)] font-mono">08:32</span>
            </button>
            <button class="chapter text-[var(--fg-dim)]">
              <span>12:19 — Cameras in low light</span>
              <span class="text-[10px] text-[var(--fg-mute)] font-mono">12:19</span>
            </button>
            <button class="chapter text-[var(--fg-dim)]">
              <span>15:41 — Verdict &amp; alternatives</span>
              <span class="text-[10px] text-[var(--fg-mute)] font-mono">15:41</span>
            </button>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <article class="article-body max-w-2xl">
        <div class="text-[10px] font-mono uppercase tracking-widest text-[var(--accent)] mb-4">
          Deep-Dive Companion · 4,200 words · 12 min read
        </div>
        <h1 class="font-display text-3xl md:text-5xl mb-7 leading-[1.02]">
          Three weeks. Eleven instruments. One conclusion about small phones.
        </h1>
        <p class="drop-cap">
          There's a particular kind of disappointment that comes from a phone that's almost right. The Aspect
          Mini Pro is the most frustrating device I've tested this year — not because it's bad, but because
          it's <em>so close</em> to being the answer to a question we've been asking for half a decade: can a
          small phone still be a flagship?
        </p>
        <div class="pull-quote">
          "It's not that the phone is perfect — it's that the compromises are finally in the right places.
          Mostly."
        </div>
        <h3>Build Quality &amp; In-Hand Feel</h3>
        <p>
          At 168g with a titanium frame, it's the first compact since the iPhone 13 Mini that doesn't feel
          like a concession. My thumb reaches the top corner. The phone disappears into a jeans pocket.
        </p>
        <h3>The Display: A Reality Check</h3>
        <p>
          On paper: 6.1" LTPO, 1–120Hz, 2400 nits peak. Measured with a Sekonic C-800, sustained outdoor
          brightness runs ~15% under the iPhone 15 Pro. Peak numbers mislead; sustained brightness is what you
          feel in sunlight.
        </p>
        <h3>The Battery Question</h3>
        <p>
          21 days averaged <strong>6 hours 42 minutes</strong> screen-on time with 1.8%/h standby drain.
          Genshin for 30 minutes costs 22% — physics wins in a chassis this small. For non-gamers: a
          non-issue.
        </p>
        <h3>The Verdict</h3>
        <p>
          Not perfect — dimmer outdoors than the best, weaker low-light than Pixel/iPhone, $899 against bigger
          rivals. And yet I keep reaching for it, because it disappears. If you've waited for a small phone
          that doesn't suck, this is it.
        </p>
        <p class="text-[var(--fg-dim)] text-sm italic mt-12 pt-6 border-t border-[var(--border)]">
          Purchased retail. No review unit, no loaner, no sponsor.{' '}
          <a href="/disclosures" class="underline">
            Full disclosures
          </a>
          .
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
          <a href="/videos/ep-247" class="btn-primary">
            Watch the video <i class="fas fa-arrow-right text-[10px]"></i>
          </a>
          <a href="/reviews" class="btn-outline">
            All reviews
          </a>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
